<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="ternatin C3 biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7280" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Ternatins are a group of 15 polyacetylated delphinidin glucosides that confer blue color to the petals of butterfly pea (|FRAME:TAX-43366|). The general structure of ternatins consists of malonylated delphinidin 3,3&apos;,5&apos;-triglucosides having 3&apos;,5&apos;-side chains with alternating D-glucose and p-coumaric acid units of varying lengths |CITS: [8991946]| |CITS:[12482461]|. Ternatins are categorized into three major groups, the A, B and D series that are characterized by either two D-glucose attached to the 3&apos; and 5&apos; position of the B-ring (A-series), a D-glucose and an acylated (p-coumaric acid) glucose chain (B-series) or two acylated glucose units (D-series) affixed to this position |CITS: [9834153]|. The variation of flower color depends on the chemical composition of the involved anthocyanins |CITS:[20054474]|. The blue flower color of butterfly-pea changes to other hues, such as mauve and white because of the lack of ternatins that contain acylated glucose units and an overall decreased content of anthocyanins |CITS:[14568080]|. The simplest ternatin is |FRAME: CPD-7864| (see |FRAME:PWY-5295|), which is the starting point for the formation of more complex ternatins, such as |FRAME:CPD-15371| (this pathway) and the most complex |FRAME:CPD-7865| and its intermediates. |FRAME:CPD-15371 Ternatin C3| is an intermediate towards the formation of |FRAME:CPD-7865| which is formed through a series of acylation and glucosylation steps, however, the actual sequence and corresponding enzymes of this pathway remain to be characterized. The enzyme catalyzing the acylation of |FRAME: CPD-7864| to form |FRAME:CPD-15371| has been purified and cloned from petals of |FRAME:TAX-43366| and shown to belong to the class of serine carboxypeptide like acyltransferases utilizing |FRAME:CPD-8674| as acyl donor |CITS:[Noda06] [Noda07]|.</Description>
